# Bayes and the screening test
subtitle: "Why a positive result from a good test can still mean only a one-in-ten chance of disease, worked out with a table and then Bayes' rule."
tags: ["probability","medical testing","bayes rule","disease screening","false positives","conditional probability"]
teaches: ["concepts/bayes-theorem","concepts/base-rate-fallacy","methods/probability-calculation","concepts/false-positive-paradox"]
thumb: "card navy"
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sulafat | a warm, clear doctor reading a short illustrated book aloud; calm, natural pace, never slow","take":"lines"}

## A thousand people tested
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}
use: two_by_two_table
with:
    row_label: Test result
    col_label: Really sick?
    row_values:
      - 'Positive: 98'
      - 'Negative: 902'
    col_values:
      - 'Sick: 10'
      - 'Healthy: 990'
    cells:
      - ['9', '89']
      - ['1', '901']
    cell_notes:
      - [true positive, false positive]
      - [false negative, true negative]

Suppose a test for a disease comes back positive. How worried should you be? To find out, test a thousand people and sort them two ways.
    draw grid row_title row_header_0 row_header_1 col_title col_header_0 col_header_1

Say one in a hundred has the disease, so ten of our thousand are sick.
    write "- **Prevalence**: 1 in 100 has the disease"

    write "- **Sensitivity**: 90 % of the sick test positive"

The test is good at finding them. It catches nine of the ten.
    draw cell_0_0

It misses one, who goes home reassured.
    draw cell_1_0

    write "- **False positives**: 9 % of the healthy"

But it also raises the alarm for nine percent of the healthy, and there are nine hundred and ninety of them. That makes eighty-nine false alarms.
    draw cell_0_1

The other nine hundred and one are rightly cleared.
    draw cell_1_1

    write id answer text "A positive: $\\dfrac{9}{9 + 89} \\approx 9\\%$ are sick"

So now read only the top row, everyone who tested positive. Ninety-eight people, and just nine of them are sick. A positive result means about a nine percent chance of disease.
    highlight cell_0_0 cell_0_1

    highlight answer effect circle

Not ninety. Nine.
    text share "9 / 98 ≈ 9 %" #b5482e x 150 y 680 font_size 40

    pause 1

## Bayes' rule
book: {"layout":"columns","look":"mixed"}

Why is it so low? Because the false alarms come from a much bigger group. Eighty-nine of them outnumber the nine true positives almost ten to one.
    text top_note "98 positives" x 300 y 635 font_size 36
    shape bar_s #b5482e shape rect x 300 y 218 width 170 height 36 style.fill #b5482e style.opacity 0.6
    shape bar_h #2f6b8f shape rect x 300 y 414 width 170 height 356 style.fill #2f6b8f style.opacity 0.35
    text l_s "9 sick" #b5482e x 470 y 218 font_size 34
    text l_h "89 healthy" #2f6b8f x 490 y 414 font_size 34
    parallel true

    write "The rare group stays small, however good the test."

However good the test is at finding the sick, there are so few of them that the healthy majority swamps them.
    highlight bar_h l_h

The rule that does it is named after the Reverend Thomas Bayes, who worked on it in the seventeen-hundreds. It turns one probability around into the other.
    write id rule text "$$P(\\text{sick} \\mid +) = \\frac{P(+ \\mid \\text{sick})\\,P(\\text{sick})}{P(+)}$$"

    highlight rule part P(\text{sick}) effect underline

And the key ingredient is the starting chance, how common the disease is before anyone is tested.
    text prior "1 in 100 sick" #b5482e x 760 y 520 font_size 34

    write "$$= \\frac{0.9 \\times 0.01}{0.098} \\approx 0.09$$"

Put our numbers in, and the rule gives the same nine percent the table did, without counting heads.
    math calc tex "\\frac{0.9 \\times 0.01}{0.098} \\approx 9\\%" size 34 x 760 y 360

    quiz "The same test is used in a clinic where half the patients are sick. Is a positive result more or less trustworthy there?"
        right: More. With half the patients sick, true positives are common, so a positive result is much more likely to be right.
        + More trustworthy
        * Less trustworthy
        * Just the same
---
